WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:08.720
At the start of the 14th century, England faced a&nbsp;
serious problem. Its armies had suffered a string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.520
of humiliating defeats at the hands of the Scots.&nbsp;
The worst being the Battle of Banukburn in 1314.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.280
There, King Edward II's troops, despite&nbsp;
outnumbering the Scottish infantry 3 to1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.880
were routed and suffered massive losses.&nbsp;
Banukburn was a disaster for England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.960
and a wake-up call that the old English&nbsp;
military system was in desperate need of change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.920
It was Edward III who finally addressed these&nbsp;
shortcomings. He reformed England's armies so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.320
profoundly and lastingly that some historians even&nbsp;
describe his reforms as a military revolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.720
The result was a war machine that proved&nbsp;
almost unbeatable on the battlefield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.800
combining the steadfastness of dismounted men at&nbsp;
arms with the deadly firepower of longbowow men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.480
In this video, we'll explore how Edward the&nbsp;
Third's reforms transformed England's armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.640
and laid the foundations for&nbsp;
the rise of the Longbow men.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.240
The basis for Edward III's reformed military&nbsp;
system was a different approach to recruitment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.600
Under Edward I and Edward II, English armies&nbsp;
consisted mainly of men raised through royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.200
commissions of array and feudal summons.&nbsp;
Vassels bound to the king by feudal obligation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.080
Edward III gradually replaced&nbsp;
his system with contract armies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.560
Forces recruited through written agreements&nbsp;
and paid full wages for their service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:47.000
These military service contracts were known&nbsp;
as indenturers. By 1359 to 1360, when Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.880
personally led an army in the field for the final&nbsp;
time, nearly his entire force had been recruited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.400
through service contracts, and feudal levies&nbsp;
had fallen out of fashion almost entirely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.560
A contract army was formed as follows. The king&nbsp;
entered into agreements with selected captains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.960
usually his vessels or other influential men, who&nbsp;
pledged to raise a retinue of a specified number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.320
of soldiers in exchange for payment. These&nbsp;
contracts outlined the length and location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:21.200
of service, rates of pay, required equipment, and&nbsp;
even details like the division of spoils of war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.920
The captains then recruited their troops,&nbsp;
either from among the men of their own estates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.240
or by making similar agreements with&nbsp;
local nobles and individual men at arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.280
Armies raised through this system offered&nbsp;
several advantages. Since the captains were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.680
responsible for recruiting their own retinues,&nbsp;
the administrative burden on the crown was reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:49.440
and it became much easier to raise multiple armies&nbsp;
at once. This was a major benefit during the 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:56.040
years war when England was often fighting on&nbsp;
several fronts in Aquitane, Britany and France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.680
as well as Scotland. The contracts also allowed&nbsp;
the king to more accurately predict how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.560
troops would be available, making the logistical&nbsp;
and strategic planning of campaigns far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:13.440
efficient. Some of the armies raised by indentures&nbsp;
were enormous. The force Edward III led during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.800
Cressy campaign is estimated to have numbered over&nbsp;
15,000 men and by the time he laid siege to Kala,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:26.040
his army had grown to around 25,000. However, this&nbsp;
was likely the largest army England fielded until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.920
the 16th century and thus is an exceptional&nbsp;
case. Most contract armies were smaller than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:38.000
those raised through feudal levies, but their&nbsp;
reduced size was offset by superior quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.600
Captains determined the size of their own&nbsp;
retinu, and since they were paid by the crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.280
they had little reason to burden the king&nbsp;
with unfit soldiers. Each captain brought men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.720
he trusted who had already proven themselves in&nbsp;
battle. As a result, troops recruited through this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.320
system were generally better trained and equipped.&nbsp;
Additionally, in contract armies, cavalry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.960
infantry were not raised separately, but rather&nbsp;
together by a single captain as one contingent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:09.600
The men in an indentured retinue usually came from&nbsp;
the same area, often even from the same village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:16.040
or hund. They knew one another, bonded through&nbsp;
service, kinship, and friendship, and often had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:21.640
fought together before. This created stronger&nbsp;
cohesion than in mixed contingents. Combined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:27.520
with reliable pay, at least in theory, this made&nbsp;
contract armies more effective and dependable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:33.400
But to reach their full potential, they lacked&nbsp;
one essential element, strong leadership.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.480
[music]

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.160
Even the most reliable troops are useless if they&nbsp;
are poorly let. And that was one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:55.360
problems of the old English armies. Edward III was&nbsp;
seen as a weak king and an ineffective general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:01.400
Edward III was the complete opposite, an&nbsp;
outstanding strategist, a quickwitted tactician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.720
and a careful, prudent leader. The nobility&nbsp;
supported him because he consistently excelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.200
at advancing the war against France, and&nbsp;
he knew how to inspire his troops while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:17.040
maintaining discipline, often by staying close to&nbsp;
the man himself. He loved fighting alongside them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.880
sometimes even in disguise, wearing the simple&nbsp;
tunic of a common man at arms. At least as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.360
important as Edward III's personal leadership&nbsp;
was the network of capable commanders he built.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.880
This was crucial because, as mentioned above, the&nbsp;
Hundred Years War was often fought on multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:40.120
fronts. Edward actively promoted military talent.&nbsp;
In 1348, he created the Order of the Guarder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.760
an elite military brotherhood that included&nbsp;
not only the highest nobility, but also knights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:50.200
and men from the lower nobility. This new&nbsp;
military elite gained invaluable experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.880
in the endless battles of the Hundred Years War.&nbsp;
And by recruiting through indentured retinu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.800
the king could choose and promote the most&nbsp;
capable captains. By the end of Edward's reign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:05.320
a professional cadre of experienced commanders&nbsp;
had emerged. a community of professionals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:11.920
able to plan complex strategies and carry them out&nbsp;
effectively. These commanders also led according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.440
to a new set of principles. They set a good&nbsp;
example, motivated their soldiers through direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.280
engagement and exhortation and maintained&nbsp;
strict discipline. An excerpt from Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.600
the third speech before the battle of Cressy&nbsp;
shows how this looked in practice. quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:36.240
"On pain of the news, none should break ranks,&nbsp;
nor seek gain, nor despoil either living or dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.320
without his leave. For if the affair went in their&nbsp;
favor, each would have enough time to pillage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.800
and if fortune turned against them, then&nbsp;
nothing they could do would gain them anything."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.200
Discipline and order often clashed with soldiers's&nbsp;
natural desire to plunder or seek personal glory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.520
in battle. Yet the English quickly learned that&nbsp;
their chances of survival improved drastically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:05.440
if they held their ground and stayed in&nbsp;
formation against the massive French armies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.520
It was essential to maintain order until the&nbsp;
commander gave the signal to plunder the infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:17.720
command [music] Havoc. These improvements at the&nbsp;
command level quickly trickled down to the ranks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:22.560
From dukes commanding entire armies to&nbsp;
humble men at arms leading small retinues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.440
the quality of leadership rose noticeably. The&nbsp;
soldiers quickly realized their leaders were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.640
competent, building trust that in turn boosted&nbsp;
morale. As the wars against the Scots and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.760
early campaigns of the Hundred Years War began to&nbsp;
bring success, English troops developed remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:44.440
self-confidence. Quote, "The English," wrote the&nbsp;
chronicler Jean Frostar, "Never let it worry them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:49.920
if they were few in numbers." Fueled by this&nbsp;
confidence and shaped by the experience in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.600
brutal early years of the war, England's&nbsp;
soldiers fought not only more skillfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:00.040
but also more resolutely. The combination of&nbsp;
good leadership and highquality contract armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:05.440
laid the groundwork of the rise of the Longbow&nbsp;
men and the emergence of a new tactical system.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.960
From the 13th century onward, English&nbsp;
armies relied heavily on longbowow men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:23.160
Until the 1320s, most fought on foot and were&nbsp;
drawn from the poor and rural population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:27.200
This began to change during the&nbsp;
military reforms of the 14th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:33.120
Edward III increasingly used mounted archers,&nbsp;
which allowed him to boost the mobility, range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.920
and tactical flexibility of his armies without&nbsp;
sacrificing the longbow's deadly firepower. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:44.960
archers still fought on foot in battle, but used&nbsp;
horses to move around quickly between positions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:50.920
The true value of mounted archers lay in their&nbsp;
mobility. They could cover large distances quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:56.040
and reposition with ease, making them&nbsp;
far more flexible than infantry archers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.760
This allowed them to respond effectively to rapid&nbsp;
maneuvers of the French cavalry and to meet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:07.680
logistical demands of long campaigns in enemy&nbsp;
territory. Most importantly, their speed made them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.520
ideal for operations based on surprise and rapid&nbsp;
movement. This gave rise to one of the tactics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:20.840
that would come to define the H 100red Years War.&nbsp;
The Shivi Shiv were raids in which mounted units&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:26.680
advanced deep into enemy territory to plunder,&nbsp;
destroy resources, and pressure the opponent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:32.160
Over time, Shio became one of the English army's&nbsp;
most powerful tools in the war against France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:39.080
Precisely because mounted archers were mobile,&nbsp;
they could also be used offensively in battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:45.040
A striking example came in 1346 when&nbsp;
Henry of Crossmont, the Earl of Derby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:50.360
rushed to relief the besieged fortress of&nbsp;
Oberash. Outside the town, Derby's forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:57.520
of 300 men at arms and 600 mounted archers, rode&nbsp;
swiftly up to the French camp of around 7,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.200
The archers took up position in front of a&nbsp;
grove to the west while most of the men at- arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:09.200
approached from the south and a small group of&nbsp;
dismounted men at arms struck from the northwest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.920
The French were completely unprepared. They&nbsp;
were sitting around their campfires having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:19.480
dinner when a hail of arrows fell upon them&nbsp;
and the men at arms charged into the camp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:25.240
With a force of infantry archers, a quick assault&nbsp;
like this would have been nearly impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:30.640
it would have taken them far too long to get into&nbsp;
position. Mounted archers proved so effective in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.120
Edward's campaign against the Scots and in&nbsp;
the early years of the Hundred Years War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:41.640
that he steadily increased their numbers. By the&nbsp;
1330s, most archers were mounted and the overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:48.080
population of archers in the army continued to&nbsp;
grow. Soon, English armies were usually roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:54.320
half men at arms and halfmounted archers, while&nbsp;
other troop types became less important. From the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:01.600
1380s on, archers even outnumbered men at arms.&nbsp;
A trend that continued in the following decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:09.680
At the famous Anakur campaign in 1415, the ratio&nbsp;
of archers to man-at-arms was about 3:1. And by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:15.920
the mid of the 15th century, it was not uncommon&nbsp;
to see 10-mounted archers for every man at arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.480
This overwhelming proportion gave English armies&nbsp;
far more firepower per soldier than any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:28.280
European army. Of course, mounted archers had&nbsp;
their drawbacks. The most obvious was cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:33.040
Horses and equipment made them far more&nbsp;
expensive than ordinary infantry archers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:39.160
and this was reflected in their pay. A mounted&nbsp;
archer typically earned around twice as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:45.320
as one of his comrades on foot. This further&nbsp;
contributed to armies becoming smaller, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:51.360
that wasn't necessarily a disadvantage. Small,&nbsp;
fast, and powerful armies proved their worth again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:57.520
and again in the early stages of the Hundred Years&nbsp;
War. Of course, having a horse wasn't enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:03.120
A mounted archer needed to know how to care for&nbsp;
it, maintain his equipment, repair his bow, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:09.800
keep himself fed and healthy on campaign. Skills&nbsp;
that took years to develop. And if you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:15.360
it, that kind of practical, hands-on knowledge is&nbsp;
something we've largely lost today. Which brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:20.960
us to today's sponsor, the book, The Ultimate&nbsp;
Guide to Rebuilding Civilization. This isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:26.640
your average survival manual. It's a beautifully&nbsp;
illustrated guide packed with practical knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:32.320
Everything from purifying water and building&nbsp;
shelter to protecting your livestock, making soap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:39.040
or even figuring out how to produce penicellin&nbsp;
or gunpowder from scratch. Every page features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:45.240
detailed handdrawn sketches that make it as&nbsp;
enjoyable to browse as it is useful to read.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:51.440
Whether you're just curious about old world skills&nbsp;
or want to be ready for anything, the book is both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:56.520
a fascinating read and a great conversation&nbsp;
piece. I've got a copy in my living room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:01.640
and it never fails to grab people's attention. You&nbsp;
can grab yours through the link in the description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:07.360
or the pinned comment. It's the one book you'll&nbsp;
