00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:15.640
[music]

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:20.200
The growing importance of mounted archers had&nbsp;
a profound impact not only on the strategic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:25.560
tactical capabilities of English armies, but also&nbsp;
on the social status of the archers themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:31.480
By the 14th century, most mounted archers no&nbsp;
longer came from the poor rural population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:36.800
Instead, they were drawn from the ymanry,&nbsp;
relatively wealthy land owners or tenants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.480
who ranked between landed gentry such as&nbsp;
knights and esquires and simple peasants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:47.640
These were men of local influence who did not hold&nbsp;
formal titles of nobility but played an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:54.680
role in their communities. This shift was closely&nbsp;
tied to wealth. Not everyone could afford a horse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.680 --> 00:14:00.400
even a simple mount for an archer. Consequently,&nbsp;
a minimum income was required to become a mounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:06.800
archer. Edward III formalized this in&nbsp;
the winter of 1344 to 45 with a decree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:15.120
Men with land worth2 or more were to serve as&nbsp;
archers. Those with 5 or more as mounted archers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:22.800
those with 10 or more as hobelars, [music] and&nbsp;
men with land worth more than £25 as men at arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:29.320
To put this into context, it's estimated that only&nbsp;
around 11,000 families in 14th century England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:37.480
had an income of more than £5 per year. The social&nbsp;
background of mounted archers was still diverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.400
ranging from the sons of landed chantry&nbsp;
to small land owners and artisans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:47.200
On average, however, they increasingly stood&nbsp;
out from ordinary infantry, if only visually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:52.560
because they could afford better equipment.&nbsp;
The social gap between man-at-arms and archers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:58.760
on the other hand, gradually became less obvious.&nbsp;
In a royal army, many archers and men at arms held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:04.120
similar social and financial status. It was not&nbsp;
uncommon, for example, for the eldest son of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:10.680
wealthy family to serve as a man-at-arms, while a&nbsp;
younger brother served as a mounted archer. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:16.440
archers were never equal to men at arms [music]&nbsp;
in role or responsibility. They always served as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:22.640
retainers and did not command their own retinues.&nbsp;
Military service also offered opportunities for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:27.280
social advancement for these new archers.&nbsp;
Just as a simple knight could earn a title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:32.720
of nobility by distinguishing himself in the wars&nbsp;
in France. A yman archer could rise to the rank of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:38.040
knight. [music] A notable example is the famous&nbsp;
condier John Hawkwood. He began his military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:43.680
career as an archer, was kned for his achievements&nbsp;
in the Hundred Years War, and eventually became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:48.480
one of the most sought-after mercenary captains&nbsp;
in Europe. You can click the info card in the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:53.520
right corner if you want to know more about John&nbsp;
Hawkwood and the Italian conductier in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.560 --> 00:16:00.640
Archers' change in social status also had&nbsp;
practical effects. England's new mounted archers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:06.080
were no longer simple peasants who went to war&nbsp;
because they had to. Some still lived modestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:13.480
but most were well-fed, healthy, and took pride in&nbsp;
their craft. They saw themselves as professionals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:19.440
skilled archers who excelled at their art.&nbsp;
This new sense of professionalism and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:25.080
closer social ties between archers and men at arms&nbsp;
made possible the single most important factor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:30.920
England's military success, the close cooperation&nbsp;
between the two. Unlike in France, where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:36.000
noble knights often looked down on the ordinary&nbsp;
infantry, the English succeeded in elevating their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:42.200
relationship between men-at-arms and archers to a&nbsp;
professional partnership. Both groups understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:47.280
that survival in battle depended on the other.&nbsp;
It was their close cooperation that allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:52.560
the longbowow men to play a central role on the&nbsp;
battlefield. Let's see how it worked in practice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:56.880
On the battlefield, the English relied on&nbsp;
a tactical system that combined the deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:02.280
firepower of Longbow men with the steadfastness of&nbsp;
dismounted men at arms. The men at arms typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:07.480
held the center while the archers took positions&nbsp;
on the wings. Together, they established a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:12.680
defensive line, reinforced with field&nbsp;
fortifications and waited for the attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:17.720
As the enemy approached, the archers unleashed a&nbsp;
hail of arrows, slowing the advance and disrupting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:23.800
the attackers's formation. While the casualties&nbsp;
were often severe, the arrows rarely stopped the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:28.920
enemy before they reached the English lines.&nbsp;
There were also blind spots in the defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:33.880
and the French quickly adapted. Beginning in&nbsp;
the 1350s, they began fighting more on foot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:38.920
wearing better plate armor and protecting&nbsp;
themselves with large shields known as pavvisas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:44.560
When the enemy reached the English lines, it was&nbsp;
the man-at-arms' moment to act. They formed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:49.880
backbone of the battle formation, the resilient&nbsp;
core around which the archers were arranged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:55.360
Their job was to halt the attack and halt the&nbsp;
line while arrows continue to rain down over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.360 --> 00:18:00.840
their heads onto the advancing enemy. As soon as&nbsp;
the enemy faltered, the men at arms mounted their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:06.400
horses, launched a swift counterattack, and&nbsp;
pursued the retreating forces. This tactical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:11.040
system was the cornerstone of English success&nbsp;
in the early battles of the Hundred Years War.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:18.120
[music]

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:22.160
The combination of dismounted men at arms&nbsp;
and mounted archers proved so effective that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:27.120
other units gradually became less important and&nbsp;
eventually disappeared from Edward III's armies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:33.240
Crossbowmen were soon used almost exclusively in&nbsp;
sieges and aboard ships while mercenary archers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:38.840
were rarely hired. The spearmen from Wales who&nbsp;
had long protected the archers were replaced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:45.800
pitfalls, earthworks, and the infamous archers'&nbsp;
stakes. Light cavalry units known as hobelars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:50.320
whose main weapon was the long lance also&nbsp;
essentially vanished from English armies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:55.880
The roles of all these units were largely&nbsp;
taken over by mounted archers who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.880 --> 00:19:01.080
in the words of historian Matthew Strickland,&nbsp;
became England's quote universal soldier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:06.360
a versatile mounted infantryman capable of&nbsp;
performing nearly any kind of engagement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:09.160
If you want to read the books&nbsp;
of Matthew Strickland yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:13.160
you can buy it through our link, which you'll&nbsp;
find down below in the description of the video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:17.640
This also supports the channel because we earn a&nbsp;
few dollars if you choose to buy through our link.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:23.960
This versatility proved invaluable in the conflict&nbsp;
of the Hundred Years War, where battles were only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:29.920
a small part of the fighting. Sieges, naval&nbsp;
operations, and shores were far more common.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:36.040
Mounted archers were ideally suited for all&nbsp;
of these since they were not just archers, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:42.440
also light cavalry capable of rapid movement and&nbsp;
flexible tactics. Mounted archers were especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:49.280
versatile during sieges, proving effective in both&nbsp;
attack and defense. They could encircle a fortress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:55.040
in an instant, secure siege lines against&nbsp;
sies and external attacks, man siege towers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.040 --> 00:20:00.040
or provide cover for man-at-arms attempting&nbsp;
to storm the walls. The effectiveness of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:06.240
covering volleys was impressively demonstrated&nbsp;
in 1345 when the Earl of Derby attacked Burgarak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:12.480
For reasons unknown, French [music] troops were&nbsp;
positioned outside the town and a battle ensued.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:17.200
Derby's archers pinned them down near&nbsp;
their camp with a dense barrage of arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:22.800
while the men at arms struck first on horseback&nbsp;
and then on foot. They put the French to flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:28.040
pursued them to the suburb of St. Medline and&nbsp;
even captured the fortified outer courtyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:32.680
on the south side of the bridge over the&nbsp;
Dordonia. Throughout the battle, the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:39.320
archers repeatedly shifted position to maintain&nbsp;
covering fire for the men at arms. Mounted archers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:45.240
were just as effective at defending towns from the&nbsp;
walls. They could keep the attackers at a distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:50.760
and create the element of surprise during&nbsp;
sorties and counterattacks. During the 1342&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:56.840
siege of Henbon in Britany, the French bombarded&nbsp;
the town for several days with a massive trebuche.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:01.360
One night, the garrison dared to make a&nbsp;
sorty to put an end to the murderous barrage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:07.680
Several dozen archers snuck up to the siege engine&nbsp;
and unleashed a hail of arrows, forcing its guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:12.880
to retreat. They then defended the position&nbsp;
while other infantry men destroyed the trebuche.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:18.080
When the French rallied and counterattacked,&nbsp;
more archers sied forth and drove them back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:24.680
Longbow men also played a crucial role in&nbsp;
amphibious operations. 2 days after the battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:30.280
of Burgarak, Derby attacked the town again, this&nbsp;
time from the slowmoving waters of the Dordonia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:36.560
The archers on his ships quote kept up so quick in&nbsp;
attack with their arrows that none dared to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:41.800
themselves unless they chose to run the risk&nbsp;
of being killed or wounded. End quote. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:47.160
Middle Ages, naval warfare was characterized by&nbsp;
a simple tactic. Ships would approach the enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:53.000
weaken them with missiles, fire and ramming, and&nbsp;
then engage in close combat to board the opponent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.880
The longbow men usually man before and after&nbsp;
castles, the super structures on the bow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:03.320
stern. From there, they forced the enemy crew&nbsp;
to take cover and provided covering fire for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:08.040
their own men at arms when they were storming&nbsp;
enemy ships or landing on a defended beach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:13.320
There are many more examples, but these provide&nbsp;
an idea of how remarkably versatile mounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:19.600
archers were. as true universal or all-purpose&nbsp;
soldiers. They gave England's armies, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:24.640
would soon consist almost entirely of archers&nbsp;
and men at arms, the flexibility to respond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:30.880
quickly and effectively to any challenge. Edward&nbsp;
III's reforms transformed England's poorly led,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:36.840
insecure, and inefficient forces in a disciplined,&nbsp;
professional, and highly effective war machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:42.360
The close cooperation between men-at-arms and&nbsp;
mounted archers became the foundation of a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:48.000
tactical system. Side by side, dismounted men-ed&nbsp;
arms and mounted archers gave English armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:56.320
a striking power that secured victories in famous&nbsp;
battles such as Cressi in 1346 and Poier in 1356,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:00.480
changing the balance of power [music]&nbsp;
in Europe for generations to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

